---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DOMAIN_MODEL_URL, GITHUB_URL, SIGN_IN_URL } from "../consts";

// Straight from the repo README's "six capabilities" list. If that list
// changes, this one does too — it is the product's own description of itself,
// not a marketing rewrite of it.
const capabilities = [
  {
    title: "Multi-step submission forms",
    body: "Abstracts from speakers, sessions from sponsors. Custom fields, saved drafts, and a deadline that actually closes.",
  },
  {
    title: "Submitter portal",
    body: "Track proposals, complete onboarding tasks, manage a public speaker profile — one place, one login.",
  },
  {
    title: "Proposal evaluation",
    body: "Rubrics, rounds, conflicts of interest and recorded decisions. Reviewers see what they should and nothing else.",
  },
  {
    title: "Speaker onboarding",
    body: "Define what accepted speakers must do — headshot, bio, slides, consent — and chase it to completion automatically.",
  },
  {
    title: "Public schedule",
    body: "An embeddable, versioned, cacheable schedule for your marketing site. Publish a snapshot; roll it back if you must.",
  },
  {
    title: "APIs and webhooks",
    body: "A scoped REST API and signed webhooks on every domain event, so the rest of your stack stays in sync.",
  },
];
---

<Base>
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Conference program management, open source.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        An alternative to SessionBoard, scoped to the jobs an AI Engineer–style conference actually
        has to get done — from the call for proposals to the schedule on the day. Not a full clone.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href={GITHUB_URL}>Get the source</a>
        <a class="btn secondary" href={SIGN_IN_URL}>Sign in</a>
      </div>

      <div class="proof">
        {/* Deliberately no test count. It was 411 when the README last said so
            and 429 a week later, and a landing page that goes stale on every
            commit is a landing page nobody updates. */}
        <p>
          Built and running. Every bounded context implemented end to end, an integration suite
          that runs against real Cloudflare bindings, a model↔code drift check that fails the
          build, and a seeded conference mid-flight you can sign into in one command.
        </p>
        <pre><code>git clone …/Podium
npm install
npm run dev</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Six capabilities</h2>
      <p class="sub">The jobs a conference has to get done, in the order it has to do them.</p>
      <div class="grid">
        {
          capabilities.map((c) => (
            <div class="card">
              <h3>{c.title}</h3>
              <p>{c.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>And the parts nobody demos</h2>
      <p class="more">
        Sponsor sessions as first-class citizens with countable entitlements. An event roster.
        Content approval with revision history. Assisted placement that respects room capacity and
        speaker clashes. Campaigns with an auditable outbox, so you can prove what was sent to whom.
        Bulk import and export. And a cross-event speaker directory, because the compounding asset
        a conference builds is not its schedule — it is the people it knows.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
          <p>
            Apache-2.0, and built for Cloudflare: Workers, D1 behind a repository layer, R2 for
            assets, KV for published snapshots, Queues for domain events, Cron Triggers for the
            sweeps, and one Durable Object per event so a schedule only ever has one writer.
          </p>
          <p>
            It deploys to a Cloudflare account you own, with your data in your D1. There is no
            hosted tier to be locked into.
          </p>
          <p><a href={GITHUB_URL}>Read the code →</a></p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2>The model is the specification</h2>
          <p>
            Entities, enums, state machines, invariants, domain events and the authorization matrix
            live in a normative domain model, and the code implements it. When the two disagree,
            that is a defect in one of them — and a check in CI that fails the build.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every open question is decided and written down, with the reasoning, including the ones
            that were decided against.
          </p>
          <p><a href={DOMAIN_MODEL_URL}>Read the domain model →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="aside">
        Unrelated to podium.com (lead management) and to <a href="https://podium-lib.io">podium-lib</a>{" "}
        (micro-frontends), both of which hold the obvious namespaces.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
